<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  Cleartext HTTP, deliberately allowed, with the reason recorded here rather than in a commit
  message nobody reads.

  The external AI backend is a URL the USER types: an Ollama or Unsloth server on their LAN or
  tailnet. It is a bare IP and port, usually with no certificate. Android has blocked cleartext
  by default since API 28 (this app targets 35), so without this file every request to that
  backend fails before it leaves the device — and the failure surfaces as "not reachable",
  which reads as the server being down while it is answering happily on another machine.

  A `domain-config` cannot fix this. Those entries are matched by hostname, and the host is
  unknown at build time; a user may point at any address on their own network. There is nothing
  to enumerate.

  So the base config permits cleartext. The compensating control is that this app only ever
  sends ONE kind of thing over that connection: a request fact summary. It carries no vault
  contents, no credentials, no SMS bodies — `RequestFacts` is already redacted before it reaches
  the backend. Tokens are sent only as the Authorization header for the server the user chose.

  Never widen this to `android:usesCleartextTraffic="true"`: that flag is the blunt version and
  hides which hosts are affected. This file is where the decision lives.
-->
<network-security-config>
    <base-config cleartextTrafficPermitted="true">
        <trust-anchors>
            <certificates src="system" />
        </trust-anchors>
    </base-config>
</network-security-config>
